    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "2b5200cf_3ce90c8d",
        "filename": "experimental/graphite/src/GlobalCache.h",
        "patchSetId": 2
      },
      "lineNbr": 18,
      "author": {
        "id": 8341
      },
      "writtenOn": "2022-02-01T16:28:57Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "I think this will be fun to figure out how to do well and efficiently. A few things come to mind to me:\n1. If we return members, like shaderCodeDictionary() currently, we\u0027ll need some way of locking the global cache externally so that the recorder ResourceProviders can lock, use, and unlock.\n2. We could instead choose to replicate/union all the member APIs onto GlobalCache and have it internally do the locking, delegation, and then unlock.\n3. We could require any class that is part of the GlobalCache to be thread safe on its own.\n4. I would bet that these will be predominantly reading from the global cache, vs. writing, so we may want to explore another thread safety mechanism than just exclusive mutexes.",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 17,
        "startChar": 0,
        "endLine": 18,
        "endChar": 78
      },
      "revId": "62926530e747985ec87ecc045c0d02a6cbf786fd",
      "serverId": "5086850b-e599-37d2-8ec8-ff16aeccbf02"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "69664689_90968645",
        "filename": "experimental/graphite/src/GlobalCache.h",
        "patchSetId": 2
      },
      "lineNbr": 18,
      "author": {
        "id": 5036
      },
      "writtenOn": "2022-02-01T16:42:04Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "FWIW I wasn\u0027t going to return shaderCodeDictionary() directly (was going to do something like 2), but then I realized that is already a thread safe class. So there was no extra thread safety that needed to be managed by the GlobalCache class itself.\n\nSo my gut feeling is that most things will fall into something like 2, but I don\u0027t think we have to use that model for everything so for things that are already thread safe, I think 3 is a fine thing as well.\n\nBut yes I agree this will be a fun little project(s) to figure out how to best optimize each use case we have.",
